# From Noisy Codewords to Quantified Security

Begin with the geometric intuition: encryption moves an encoded message through a Hamming cube by adding a sparse error. Explore the identity between movement and error weight directly.

{{interactive_demo:0}}

{{demo:0}}

<details><summary>Reveal the correctness theorem and proof idea</summary>

If distinct encoded words have Hamming distance at least $2t+1$, their radius-$t$ balls are disjoint. Since adding an error of weight at most $t$ places the ciphertext in the transmitted word's ball, no different encoded word can also lie within radius $t$. An injective encoder therefore recovers the unique message.

For broader background, see [error-correcting codes](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Error_correction_code) and [Hamming distance](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hamming_distance).
</details>

