<details><summary>Reveal the scope and the unresolved complexity question</summary>

If $N\ge2^{256}$ and $q<2^{128}$, then $q^2<2^{256}\le N$. This conclusion assumes the attack is governed by the condition $q^2<N$. It does not prove that all quantum attacks are unstructured. Nor do these results prove that distinguishing binary Goppa codes from random linear codes is NP-hard. Worst-case hardness of generic syndrome decoding does not automatically imply average-case key indistinguishability or IND-CPA security.

For context, explore [the McEliece cryptosystem](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/McEliece_cryptosystem) and [Grover's algorithm](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Grover%27s_algorithm).
</details>
